  csp: "# Allow Subtext in the Content-Security-Policy\n\nThe app has a CSP. Add these sources to it, editing the policy where it's defined. Keep every existing source.\n\nUS orgs (`-na1`):\n\n- `script-src`: `https://edge.fullstory.com https://rs.fullstory.com`\n- `connect-src`: `https://edge.fullstory.com https://rs.fullstory.com`\n- `img-src`: `https://rs.fullstory.com`\n\nEU orgs (`-eu1`): use `edge.eu1.fullstory.com` and `rs.eu1.fullstory.com` instead.\n\nThe snippet starts with an inline script, so `script-src` also needs `'unsafe-inline'` or a nonce on the snippet's `<script>` tag. If the app already uses nonces, use the same mechanism. Don't add `'unsafe-inline'` to a policy that doesn't have it without asking the user.\n\nIf the policy is set in a hosting dashboard you can't edit, tell the user exactly which directives to change.\n\nDetails and per-platform examples: https://subtext.fullstory.com/api/csp.md\n",
  identify: "# Identify users\n\nTie each session to the signed-in user so sessions can be found by user.\n\n1. Find where the authenticated user becomes available on the client: login handler, auth provider, session bootstrap, or similar. Use only data already on the client; don't add API calls.\n2. Call `FS('setIdentity')` there, only for signed-in users:\n\n        FS('setIdentity', {\n          uid: user.id,\n          properties: { displayName: user.name, email: user.email },\n        });\n\n   - `uid` is the stable internal user id, never the email.\n   - Add other user properties the client already has, if useful.\n3. In React, call it inside a `useEffect` keyed on the user, not on every render or at module top level:\n\n        useEffect(() => {\n          if (!user) return;\n          FS('setIdentity', { uid: user.id, properties: { displayName: user.name, email: user.email } });\n        }, [user]);\n\nIf you can't find where the user is known, or the app has no sign-in, tell the user and skip this page.\n\nDetails: https://subtext.fullstory.com/api/identification.md\n",
  integrations: "# Link sessions into existing tools\n\nIf the app already uses analytics, error-tracking, or feature-flag tools, attach the Subtext session URL to them. Then anyone looking at a user or error in that tool can jump to the session.\n\n## 1. Find what's installed\n\nCheck `package.json` (and script tags) for tools like PostHog, Segment, Amplitude, Mixpanel, Sentry, Datadog, LogRocket, Statsig, LaunchDarkly, GrowthBook, Intercom, Pendo, or Appcues. Also look for a shared analytics wrapper under `lib/`, `utils/`, or `analytics/`. Don't install new tools.\n\nIf none are installed, skip to Next.\n\n## 2. Attach the URL\n\nGet the URL once capture has started:\n\n    const subtextUrl = FS('getSession', { format: 'url.now' });\n\nSend it to each tool under the key `subtext_url`, next to where that tool already identifies the user. For example:\n\n    posthog.identify(user.id, { subtext_url: subtextUrl });\n    Sentry.setTag('subtext_url', subtextUrl);\n\nIf there's a shared wrapper, attach it there once. Re-attach when the user is re-identified, since the URL changes between sessions.\n\nThe exact call for each tool is in its guide: https://subtext.fullstory.com/guides/analytics-linkage/index.md\n",
  privacy: "# Mask sensitive data\n\nCapture records DOM content, and form inputs are masked by default. Tag anything else that shows personal or sensitive data before the user deploys.\n\n## 1. Find sensitive elements\n\nLook at profile, settings, account, billing, checkout, and messaging screens for elements that render:\n\n- emails, phone numbers, addresses, names, usernames, profile photos\n- payment data, account numbers, balances\n- passwords, MFA codes, recovery phrases\n- government IDs, health data\n- free-text fields where users might paste any of the above\n\n## 2. Tag them\n\nAdd a class to the element:\n\n- `fs-mask`: keep the layout, hide the text. Use for most PII.\n- `fs-exclude`: drop the element from capture entirely. Use for payment fields and secrets.\n- `fs-unmask`: re-allow capture inside a masked or excluded parent.\n\nIf the app has a consent flow, use the `-without-consent` variants (`fs-mask-without-consent`, `fs-exclude-without-consent`) instead.\n\nShow the user the list of elements and the class you picked for each before editing, unless they told you to go ahead. Check the markup still compiles.\n\nThese tags only apply to sessions captured after the deploy.\n\nDetails: https://subtext.fullstory.com/guides/sensitive-elements.md and https://subtext.fullstory.com/api/privacy-classes.md\n",
